WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Injection happens when untrusted input is interpreted as instructions in the wrong context.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:33.640
For C# developers, that context includes database queries, ORM raw SQL, NoSQL filters, LDAP filters, XML and XPath processing, OData options, dynamic LINQ, shell commands, templates, expression evaluators, unsafe deserialization paths, browser output, file paths, URLs, message systems, and downstream APIs.

3
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.700
The category is broad because modern applications depend on many interpreters.

4
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.420
Strong prevention starts with data flow awareness.

5
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:55.960
Know where input enters the system, how it moves through controllers, binders, DTOs, services, repositories, renderers, parsers, and outbound clients, and where it reaches a sensitive sink.

6
00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.460
Convert raw input into typed, constrained values.

7
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.200
Use allowlists for fields and operations.

8
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.060
Prefer safe APIs that separate data from syntax.

9
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:14.360
Validate shape and intent before use, but do not confuse validation with parameterization or output encoding.

10
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.280
The right control depends on the sink.

11
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.940
Database code needs parameterized commands or safe ORM usage.

12
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.560
Query endpoints need bounded field and operator choices.

13
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.140
Process execution needs controlled executables and arguments.

14
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.760
Templates and expression engines need separation between user content and executable logic.

15
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.900
Parsers need safe modes, size limits, and request-specific models.

16
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.280
Browser output needs context-specific encoding and careful treatment of raw markup.

17
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.880
The goal is not memorizing payloads.

18
00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:55.140
The professional goal is designing .NET applications where untrusted data cannot change command meaning.

19
00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:05.320
That means secure defaults, narrow adapters, least privilege, dependency review, focused tests, and code review questions that make risky transitions visible.

20
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:12.300
When these practices become part of normal development, injection prevention becomes a design habit rather than a cleanup project.
